NXR-051|Ethernet adapter boot contract|Networking|The Ethernet adapter shall initialize once at boot with complete MAC/input callbacks and preserve state on invalid or repeated initialization.|Existing callbacks provide a small reusable integration seam.|Missing callbacks and second initialization fail unchanged; before initialization link is false, poll returns zero and send errors; callback context remains valid for adapter lifetime.|Initialization boundaries and target callback-lifetime review.|NXR-001,NXR-002|Optional RTOS_ETHERNET; fixed adapter instance count.|One permanent 1536-byte RX scratch buffer and fixed callback/statistics state per instance.|include/rtos_ethernet.hpp,src/ethernet.cpp,tests/ethernet.cpp|initialize validates callbacks and repeats; host tests cover selected cases but board integration is absent.|include/rtos_ethernet.hpp,src/ethernet.cpp|tests/ethernet.cpp|PartialSourceUnverified
NXR-052|Bounded Ethernet RX validation|Networking|RX polling shall attempt at most its supplied budget, stop on empty/busy/error and reject frames outside 14..1536 bytes before protocol delivery.|Work budgets and bounds protect the worker and scratch buffer.|Budget zero invokes no receive; N budget invokes at most N receives; malformed lengths never reach input; hardware callbacks never write beyond supplied capacity; errors terminate the call.|Budget/length boundaries, malformed frames and guarded-buffer MAC tests.|NXR-051|Per-call receive budget; standard/tagged frames without FCS; no jumbo support.|One fixed scratch frame; no per-frame allocation; callback execution independently bounded.|src/ethernet.cpp,include/rtos_ethernet.hpp,tests/ethernet.cpp|poll bounds attempts and validates lengths; host tests cannot establish third-party MAC buffer safety.|src/ethernet.cpp,include/rtos_ethernet.hpp|tests/ethernet.cpp|PartialSourceUnverified
NXR-053|Ethernet TX staging and backpressure|Networking|Transmit shall consume/copy valid caller bytes before return, return busy without retaining caller pointers and use boot-reserved staging for asynchronous hardware.|Caller buffers cannot remain DMA-owned after synchronous return.|Null/invalid lengths error; accepted send permits immediate caller reuse; busy consumes no caller ownership; full staging returns busy; completion returns each slot exactly once.|Caller overwrite, staging exhaustion, duplicate/late completion and retry tests.|NXR-048,NXR-051|Configured TX staging slots and maximum 1536-byte frame.|Fixed staging frames and completion metadata; no pool growth.|include/rtos_ethernet.hpp,src/ethernet.cpp,tests/ethernet.cpp|send forwards synchronously; consumption is a callback contract, not enforced hardware behavior.|include/rtos_ethernet.hpp,src/ethernet.cpp|tests/ethernet.cpp|PartialSourceUnverified
NXR-054|DMA ring ownership and coherency|Networking|MAC DMA shall use aligned boot-reserved rings with free, hardware-owned, completed and quarantined states and board-provided cache/barrier operations.|Static buffers alone do not ensure coherency or prevent double reuse.|Every descriptor has one owner; handoff barriers precede ownership bits; cache operations respect lines; duplicate completion faults; abort failure quarantines until reset quiescence.|DMA ownership model, barrier instrumentation and saturation/reset tests.|NXR-048,NXR-053|Board alignment/cache requirements and fixed RX/TX descriptor counts.|Fixed aligned rings and packet storage; ownership-state counts equal configured slots.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/memory.rst,include/rtos_ethernet.hpp|DMA/cache responsibility belongs to MAC; no rings or board barriers are implemented.
NXR-055|PHY and MDIO deadlines|Networking|PHY management shall perform finite MDIO transactions and deadline-bounded reset/autonegotiation, returning unavailable on unsupported identity or deadline expiration.|Negotiation must not stall the network worker indefinitely.|Stuck-busy transactions expire; invalid address fails before access; reset/negotiation respect absolute deadlines; unsupported PHY never enables traffic.|Busy, missing-PHY, identity and negotiation timeout tests.|NXR-009,NXR-026,NXR-027|Optional PHY; fixed address and supported identity table.|Fixed transaction state and one sampled link record per interface.|docs/drivers.rst,src/ethernet.cpp|PHY/MDIO is listed for external PHY but link_up is only a callback.
NXR-056|Link transition state machine|Networking|The network owner shall process down, negotiating, up and fault transitions, reject new TX while down and quiesce ownership before recycling buffers.|Cable loss must not strand descriptors or expose stale configuration.|Down rejects send; pending TX completes/errors once; repeated flaps conserve slots; stale generations fail; link reads never wait for negotiation.|Cable flap, pending-TX loss, stale completion and recovery tests.|NXR-054,NXR-055|Fixed interfaces and bounded transition/recovery deadlines.|One fixed state/generation record per interface; no reconnect allocation.|docs/drivers.rst,src/ethernet.cpp,include/rtos_ethernet.hpp|link_up queries a callback but no transition or outstanding-I/O cleanup exists.
NXR-057|Single network-owner worker|Networking|Each stack instance shall confine MAC polling, protocol entry, timers and stack statistics to one boot-created worker; other threads shall submit bounded requests.|The adapter prohibits concurrent and reentrant calls.|Wrong-worker/recursive entry is rejected or trapped in development; ISR only signals work; full requests return busy; each loop services bounded RX, requests and timers without starvation.|Owner/reentrancy misuse, concurrent requests and bounded fairness tests.|NXR-015,NXR-022,NXR-051|Fixed worker priority/stack and per-loop RX/request/timer budgets.|One boot stack plus fixed request/completion queues per stack.|include/rtos_ethernet.hpp,docs/drivers.rst,src/ethernet.cpp|Ownership is documented but not enforced; no protocol worker/mailbox exists.
NXR-058|Existing stack integration seam|Networking|An optional existing TCP/IP stack shall integrate behind a version-pinned C/C++ adapter with boot-reserved pools and audited ownership paths.|Reuse networking without weakening allocation constraints.|Chosen version/config cross-builds; forbidden allocator symbols absent; each retained packet has bounded owner/release paths; disabling stack removes code/pools.|Pinned-stack build, negative allocation fixture and ownership audit.|NXR-002,NXR-057|Optional raw single-owner stack initially; no blanket POSIX claim.|Explicit pool/table budgets; link fits selected board RAM; no claim full networking fits default RAM.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/memory.rst,ports/riscue/link.ld|Documentation suggests lwIP-like ownership but no stack sources/glue are linked.
NXR-059|Protocol timer servicing|Networking|The network worker shall service protocol deadlines from monotonic kernel time with a finite due-work budget each iteration and no timer reset.|RX floods must not starve retransmission or leases.|Wrap-safe due times; floods cannot postpone timers beyond configured service budget; excess work is counted and continued next loop; callbacks only in owner context.|Flood, timer burst, wrap and owner-context tests.|NXR-009,NXR-012,NXR-057,NXR-058|Fixed maximum timers and per-loop timer budget.|Boot timer records; at most configured callbacks per loop.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/architecture.rst,src/kernel.cpp|Stack timer service is guidance only; mtime must remain monotonic.
NXR-060|Packet pool conservation|Networking|Packet storage shall use boot-reserved pools with bounded runtime loans, validated ownership transitions and explicit exhaustion results.|A fixed arena can still leak loans.|Free+loaned+hardware+quarantined counts equal capacity; double release/foreign pointers fail; exhaustion drops/rejects with counters; stress recovers every nonquarantined loan.|Exhaustion, double release, foreign-pointer and conservation stress tests.|NXR-002,NXR-054,NXR-058|Fixed block sizes/counts and maximum chain length.|Exact pool bytes; no fallback allocator or unbounded chains.|docs/memory.rst,docs/drivers.rst|Docs warn static backing does not prove leak freedom; no packet pool exists.
NXR-061|Bounded socket-like endpoints|Networking|Optional socket-like endpoints shall borrow generation-tagged boot slots, route calls through the owner worker and return timeout, closed and exhausted outcomes.|Connections must reuse static resources.|Exhaustion immediate; stale handles fail; closing rejects new requests and frees only after quiescence; timed-out callers regain buffers only after ownership resolution.|Exhaustion, stale ID, close/request races and timeout ownership tests.|NXR-028,NXR-029,NXR-057,NXR-060|Optional endpoint subset; fixed endpoints/requests.|Fixed endpoint records and staging; bounded loans allowed.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/memory.rst,include/rtos_ethernet.hpp|Sockets/glue are absent; descriptor generations can bound connection slots.
NXR-062|Optional IPv4 baseline|Networking|An IPv4 profile shall support static address, netmask, gateway and bounded ARP while rejecting malformed lengths/checksums before delivery.|The minimal network profile needs a finite baseline.|Reject invalid address/mask combinations; malformed IPv4/ARP preserves unrelated state; ARP full evicts oldest-expired then oldest entry; fragmentation initially rejected/counted.|Static routing, ARP capacity/expiry and malformed packet tests.|NXR-058,NXR-059,NXR-060|Optional IPv4; fixed ARP entries; fragmentation/reassembly disabled initially.|Fixed ARP/routes; no fragment buffers; board total-memory gate.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/configuration.rst|No IP/addressing profile exists; bounded optional settings are needed.
NXR-063|Bounded DHCP client|Networking|Optional DHCP shall use fixed per-interface lease state, capped retries/backoff and remove expired addresses before further application traffic.|Automatic addressing must not retain stale leases or block indefinitely.|Retry/backoff maxima respected; acquisition deadline reports no-address; expiry removes address; link loss pauses acquisition; malformed options do not overrun buffers.|Lease acquire/renew/expire, absent server, flaps and malformed options tests.|NXR-056,NXR-059,NXR-062,NXR-066|Optional DHCPv4; fixed transactions, retry ceiling and acquisition deadline.|One fixed client state and bounded message buffer/interface.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/configuration.rst|DHCP is explicitly absent; storage and retry semantics are unspecified.
NXR-064|Bounded DNS client|Networking|Optional DNS shall use bounded names, fixed query/cache slots, response deadlines and bounded compression decoding.|Names introduce variable input and recursive-parser risks.|Overlong names fail before send; full table returns busy; reply ID/source/question match; loops/depth excess fail; timeout releases once; cache evicts oldest-expired then oldest.|Query/cache exhaustion, timeout, mismatches and compression-loop corpus.|NXR-059,NXR-062,NXR-066|Optional A records initially; fixed names/queries/cache and decode-step limit.|Fixed query/cache/message bytes; no recursive allocation.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/memory.rst|DNS is unimplemented with no parser or query ownership.
NXR-065|Bounded TCP service|Networking|Optional TCP shall enforce fixed connection, send/receive and retransmission budgets, expose partial progress and terminate retry exhaustion without leaking loans.|Streams otherwise retain scarce resources indefinitely.|Connection/byte caps report pressure; loss retransmits within ceiling; timeout/reset/close releases or quarantines every loan; EOF/half-close differ from would-block.|Loss/reorder, capacity, partial I/O, retry exhaustion and close conservation tests.|NXR-059,NXR-060,NXR-061,NXR-062|Optional TCP; fixed windows/segments/retries and close deadline.|Fixed control blocks and windows; profiles may need larger board RAM.|docs/drivers.rst,docs/memory.rst,ports/riscue/link.ld|No TCP implementation exists; default RAM cannot imply arbitrary windows fit.
NXR-066|Bounded UDP datagrams|Networking|Optional UDP shall preserve datagram boundaries in fixed endpoint/RX slots, drop newest on full queues and report truncation explicitly.|Datagrams need finite overflow semantics distinct from streams.|One receive consumes one datagram; short destination reports copied/original lengths and discards remainder; full counts drop; malformed checksum/length rejects; send pressure preserves caller ownership.|Boundary/truncation, queue full, checksum and send-pressure tests.|NXR-059,NXR-060,NXR-061,NXR-062|Optional UDP; fixed endpoints, queued datagrams and payload maximum.|Fixed slots backed by packet loans; no growth.|docs/drivers.rst,include/rtos_ethernet.hpp|Current callbacks carry frames only; no UDP lifecycle exists.
